$|X|$", "MATH_0044": "$|U| \\to |X|$", "MATH_0045": "$u_n$", "MATH_0046": "$x_0$", "MATH_0047": "$|X|$", "MATH_0048": "$\\{u_n\\}$", "MATH_0049": "$|U|$", "MATH_0050": "$x_0$", "MATH_0051": "$|X|$", "MATH_0052": "$|X|$", "MATH_0053": "$|X|$", "MATH_0054": "$x_0$", "MATH_0055": "$|X|$", "MATH_0056": "$x_0$", "MATH_0057": "$S \\to X$", "MATH_0058": "$X \\to S$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{algebra-lemma-quasi-compact}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{algebra-lemma-idempotent-spec}", "REF_0003": "\\ref{spaces-properties-section-points}", "REF_0004": "\\ref{morphisms-lemma-algebraic-residue-field-extension-closed-point-fibre}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\begin{example}", "STRUCT_0002": "\\medskip", "STRUCT_0003": "\\noindent", "STRUCT_0004": "\\end{example}"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:3b4a3bf22f1756970377595c9d7005fa154568497c2113b507a34995eebd94d8", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:9827ea5e8c1c26869b9bb234abe60cbd6202e281320afd97a3bc6911702a0f99", "source_text": "<STRUCT_0001>\n<LOCKED_0001>\nLet <MATH_0001> be a field.\nLet <MATH_0002> be the infinite product.\nSet <MATH_0003> seen as a scheme over <MATH_0004>.\nNote that the projection maps <MATH_0005> define open\nand closed immersions <MATH_0006>. Set\n<MATH_0007>\nwith morphism <MATH_0008> equal to <MATH_0009> on the component <MATH_0010>\nand <MATH_0011> on the component <MATH_0012> corresponding to <MATH_0013>.\nIt is clear from the remark above that <MATH_0014> are <ACCENT_0001>tale.\nIt is also clear that <MATH_0015> is an equivalence relation. Hence we\nobtain an algebraic space\n<MATH_0016>\nTo see what this means we specialize to the case where\nthe field <MATH_0017> is finite with <MATH_0018> elements. Let us first\ndiscuss the topological space <MATH_0019> associated to the scheme <MATH_0020>\na little bit. All elements of <MATH_0021> satisfy <MATH_0022>.\nHence every residue field of <MATH_0023> is isomorphic to <MATH_0024>, and\nall points of <MATH_0025> are closed. But the topology on <MATH_0026> isn't\nthe discrete topology. Let <MATH_0027> be the point corresponding\nto <MATH_0028>. As mentioned above the points <MATH_0029> are\nthe open points (and hence isolated). This implies there have\nto be other points since we know <MATH_0030> is quasi-compact, see\nAlgebra, Lemma <REF_0001>\n(hence not equal to an infinite discrete set).\nAnother way to see this is because the (proper) ideal\n<MATH_0031>\nis contained in a maximal ideal. Note also that every element\n<MATH_0032> of <MATH_0033> is of the form <MATH_0034> where <MATH_0035> is a unit and <MATH_0036> is an\nidempotent. Hence a basis for the topology of <MATH_0037> consists of open and\nclosed subsets (see Algebra, Lemma <REF_0002>.)\nSo the topology on <MATH_0038> is totally disconnected, but nontrivial.\nFinally, note that <MATH_0039> is dense in <MATH_0040>.\n\n<STRUCT_0002><STRUCT_0003>\nWe will later define a topological space <MATH_0041> associated to <MATH_0042>, see\nProperties of Spaces, Section <REF_0003>.\nWhat can we say about <MATH_0043>?\nIt turns out that the map <MATH_0044> is surjective and continuous.\nAll the points <MATH_0045> map to the same point <MATH_0046> of <MATH_0047>, and none of\nthe other points get identified. Since <MATH_0048> is dense in <MATH_0049> we\nconclude that the closure of <MATH_0050> in <MATH_0051> is <MATH_0052>. In other words\n<MATH_0053> is irreducible and <MATH_0054> is a generic point of <MATH_0055>. This seems\nbizarre since also <MATH_0056> is the image of a section\n<MATH_0057> of the structure morphism <MATH_0058> (and in the case of\nschemes this would imply it was a closed point, see\nMorphisms, Lemma\n<REF_0004>).\n<STRUCT_0004>", "source_text_hash": "sha256:7a3a4a52790e33af1f317355724ffd1e46992e1405b5b3e285e38fc7fcc7bebb", "unit_id": "inventory:spaces:02Z8:example/0001"}}}, {"context_id": "locked-English-neighb